{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2516 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "266dd19540d94a4b954d95d9cf6c11bc6d3d2558f5e55a30d70322ae398014da" :edb/db-before-t 2515 :edb/db-after-t 2516 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535828 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:19:36.956Z" 13194139535828 true] [17592186128830 1100 "Given the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1)$." 13194139535828 false] [17592186128830 1100 "Given the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2,$ find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1).$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186128830 1145 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0,z_0) =(2,-2,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}i + f_{y}j + f_{z}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})k \\\\\n&= 2xi + 2yj + 2zk \\\\\n∇f(2,-2, 1) &= 2(2)i + 2(-2)j + 2(1)k \\\\\n&= 4i - 4j + 2k \\\\\n&= ⟨4,-4, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}) \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n∇f(2,-2, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y - (-2), z - 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨4,-4, 2⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y + 2, z - 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n4(x - 2) - 4(y + 2) + 2(z - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 4y + 2z - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 2y + z - 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535828 false] [17592186128830 1145 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(x_0,y_0,z_0) =(2,-2,1)\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,-2,1) & =2(2)\\text{ }i+2(-2)\\text{ }j+2(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j+2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-4\\,,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,-2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-(-2),\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,\\,-4,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y+2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)-4(y+2)+2(z-1) & =0 \\\\ 4x-4y+2z-18 & =0 \\\\ 2x-2y+z-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186128933 1100 "Consider the surface $z^3y^2 =x^2-5$. Find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at the point $(-1,2,-1)$." 13194139535828 false] [17592186128933 1100 "Consider the surface $z^3y^2 =x^2-5.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at the point $(-1,2,-1).$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186128933 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{3}y^{2} &= x^{2} - 5 \\\\\nz^{3}y^{2} - x^{2} &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = z^3y^2 - x^2\n$$\n\nour surface can be viewed as the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=-5\n$$\n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}(x, y, z)i + f_{y}(x, y, z)j + f_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z^{3}y^{2} - x^{2})k \\\\\n&= (-2x)i + 2z^{3}yj + 3z^{2}y^{2}k \\\\\n∇f(-1, 2,-1) &= (-2)(-1)i + 2(-1)^{3}(2)j + 3(-1)^{2}(2)^{2}k \\\\\n&= 2i - 4j + 12k \\\\\n&= ⟨2,-4, 12⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}) \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n∇f(-1, 2,-1) \\cdot ⟨x - (-1), y - 2, z - (-1)⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨2,-4, 12⟩ \\cdot ⟨x + 1, y - 2, z + 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n2(x + 1) - 4(y - 2) + 12(z + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 4y + 12z + 22 &= 0 \\\\\nx - 2y + 6z + 11 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535828 false] [17592186128933 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{3}{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = z^3y^2 - x^2,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-5.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2,-1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(-2x)\\text{ }i+2{z}^{3}y\\text{ }j+3{z}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,2,-1) & =(-2)(-1)\\text{ }i+2(-1)^{3}(2)\\text{ }j+3(-1)^{2}(2)^{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-4j+12\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-4,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,2,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-2,\\,z-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-4,12⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-2,\\,z+1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2(x+1)-4(y-2)+12(z+1) & =0 \\\\ 2x-4y+12z+22 & =0 \\\\ x-2y+6z+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186129014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368667 13194139535828 true] [17592186129014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368681 13194139535828 true] [17592186129014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368688 13194139535828 true] [17592186129014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368695 13194139535828 true] [17592186129014 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368702 13194139535828 true] [17592186129114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368653 13194139535828 true] [17592186129114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368660 13194139535828 true] [17592186129114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368674 13194139535828 true] [17592186129114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368709 13194139535828 true] [17592186129550 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535828 true] [17592186129550 1100 "Consider the surface $\\ln (2x^{3} + yz) = 3$. Given that $n = ⟨a, b, c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(e, e^{2},-e)$, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?" 13194139535828 false] [17592186129550 1100 "Consider the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz)=3.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(e,{e}^{2},-e),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186129550 1145 "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(e,{e}^{2},-e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot 6{x}^{2})i+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot z)j+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{z}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(e,{e}^{2},-e) & =\\frac{6{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(-e)}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }j+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{e}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{e}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{e})}{(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}=-6e.$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368653 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368654 13194139535828 true] [17592186368653 1089 "q-102711" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368653 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535828 true] [17592186368653 1100 "Consider the surface $xy{z}^{2}=6-z.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(1,1,2).$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368653 1113 #uuid "58e862d4-ec1e-593c-943b-eb5a01ae4391" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368653 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{z}^{2} & =6-z \\\\ xy{z}^{2}+z & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{z}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+(x{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2xyz+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1,2) & =(1)(2)^{2}\\text{ }i+(1)(2)^{2}\\text{ }j+(2(1)(1)(2)+1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i+4j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨4,4,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,4,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-1)+4(y-1)+5(z-2) & =0 \\\\ 4x+4y+5z-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368655 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368656 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368657 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368658 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368659 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1041 "selection" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368659 13194139535828 true] [17592186368654 1110 #uuid "8ccf80b9-7cda-5bb3-8597-0bf687682fd5" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368655 1034 "4x+3y+z+12=0" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535828 true] [17592186368655 1111 #uuid "fd142c72-f441-519a-a7d9-da7c1727a520" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368656 1034 "4x-y+2z-10=0" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535828 true] [17592186368656 1111 #uuid "8d22379f-ff10-5dba-9bcb-c870b0b45323" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368657 1034 "x+4y-3z+15=0" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368657 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535828 true] [17592186368657 1111 #uuid "6b7a9547-6d52-54e9-9aab-3d6b32281afb" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368658 1034 "2x-y+5z-12=0" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535828 true] [17592186368658 1111 #uuid "563941e3-a9cc-5331-a246-1a6373b6d27b" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368659 1034 "4x+4y+5z-18=0" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535828 true] [17592186368659 1111 #uuid "4ca43c0d-7fe2-5755-87ef-42cf6289c5c4" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368660 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368661 13194139535828 true] [17592186368660 1089 "q-102718" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368660 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535828 true] [17592186368660 1100 "Consider the surface $z{e}^{x}+xy={x}^{2}.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(-1,1,2e).$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368660 1113 #uuid "61373bd7-6020-5bb2-b647-c3731f4fac66" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368660 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z{e}^{x}+xy & ={x}^{2} \\\\ z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,1,2e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{e}^{x}+xy-{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}+y-2x)\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j+{e}^{x}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,1,2e) & =((2e){e}^{-1}+1-2(-1))\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+{e}^{-1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5\\,i-j+{e}^{-1}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨5,-1,{e}^{-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,2e)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2e⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨5,-1,{e}^{-1}⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2e⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5(x+1)+(-1)(y-1)+{e}^{-1}(z-2e) & =0 \\\\ 5x-y+{e}^{-1}z+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535828 true] [17592186368661 1000 #edb/re